1 @ G)).sum(dim=-1)\n# \n# Cost: G = x@x''^T (B*L*L*C FLOPs) + p@G (B*H*L*L*L FLOPs) * 2\n# Alternative: p@x directly, then norm: p@x (B*H*L*L*C FLOPs) * 2 + norm (B*H*L*C)\n# For case 4: Gram = 84M + 2*335M = 754M vs 2*1680M = 3360M\n# Gram is way better for large C.\n\n# What about computing only u1_norm and using algebra for m_norm?\n# m = (1-beta)*u0 + beta*u1\n# ||m||''^2 = (1-beta)''^2*||u0||''^2 + beta''^2*||u1||''^2 + 2*beta*(1-beta)*u0.u1\n# We need ||u0||''^2 and u0.u1 too. Still need p0@G. Same cost.\n\n# NEW IDEA: Instead of Gram, can we use XTX form and batch it differently?\n# G = x''^T @ x is C x C. Then ||p@x||''^2 = sum over c of (p@x)_c''^2 = trace(x''^T p''^T p x)... no.\n# Actually: ||(p @ x)_i||''^2 = sum_c (sum_j p_ij x_jc)''^2 = sum_j sum_k p_ij p_ik sum_c x_jc x_kc\n# That'\"'s the same as before with G_jk.\n# \n# What if we compute it as: for each row p_i of p, norm = ||p_i @ x||_2\n# p_i is (L,), x is (L,C). So p_i @ x is (C,). norm is scalar.\n# We do this B*H*L times. That's B*H*L * L*C = B*H*L\"'^2*C FLOPs total.\n# Same as p@x (which is one big matmul).\n# \n# Gram approach: G = x @ x''^T is L x L (cost: L''^2*C), then p @ G is B*H*L*L*L, then multiply-sum p.\n# Total: L''^2*C + B*H*L''^3 + B*H*L''^2 = L''^2*C + B*H*L''^3 (approximately)\n# Direct p@x approach: B*H*L''^2*C\n# \n# Ratio: (L''^2*C + B*H*L''^3) / (B*H*L''^2*C) = 1/(B*H) + L/C\n# For case 4: 1/20 + 256/1280 = 0.05 + 0.2 = 0.25. Gram is 4x better!\n# For case 1: 1/8 + 64/512 = 0.125 + 0.125 = 0.25. Gram is 4x better.\n# For case 5: 1/10 + 256/640 = 0.1 + 0.4 = 0.5. Gram is 2x better.\n# \n# So Gram is always better. Good.\n\n# Now, the biggest cost besides projections is: \n# 1. p@G (2 of them) - up to 30% of FLOPs\n# 2. softmax (2 of them) - surprisingly expensive\n# \n# Let'\"'s see if we can reduce the p@G cost with better memory layout.\n\np = torch.randn(B, H, L, L).contiguous()\nG = torch.randn(B, L, L)\n\n# p @ G_exp where G_exp = G.unsqueeze(1) -> broadcasting\nG_exp = G.unsqueeze(1)\nbench('p @ G_exp (4D broadcast)', lambda: p @ G_exp)\n\n# Reshape to (B*H, L, L) @ (B, L, L) -> need to expand G\np3 = p.reshape(B*H, L, L)\nG_exp3 = G.unsqueeze(1).expand(B, H, L, L).reshape(B*H, L, L)\nbench('p3 @ G_exp3 (3D, reshaped expand)', lambda: p3 @ G_exp3)\n\n# What about using bmm?\nbench('torch.bmm (3D)', lambda: torch.bmm(p3, G_exp3))\n\n# What about: compute p1@G once, then use for both norm1 and part of normm?\n# We already do this (compute p1G for norm1_sq and also could use for cross term).\n# But normm currently uses pmix@G which is a separate matmul.\n\n# Key idea: Can we replace pmix@G with a combination of p0@G and p1@G?\n# pmix = (1-beta)*p0 + beta*p1\n# pmix @ G = (1-beta)*(p0 @ G) + beta*(p1 @ G)\n# Then normm_sq = (pmix * (pmix @ G)).sum(dim=-1)\n# = sum_j [((1-b)*p0_j + b*p1_j) * ((1-b)*(p0@G)_j + b*(p1@G)_j)]\n# = (1-b)\"'^2 * sum(p0 * (p0@G)) + b''^2 * sum(p1 * (p1@G)) + 2*b*(1-b) * sum(p0 * (p1@G))\n# = (1-b)''^2 * norm0_sq + b''^2 * norm1_sq + 2*b*(1-b) * cross\n# where cross = (p0 * (p1@G)).sum(dim=-1)\n# \n# This replaces 1 p@G matmul (pmix@G) with:\n# - 1 p@G matmul (p0@G) - same number of matmuls!\n# - some elementwise ops\n# Wait, we still need 2 matmuls (p1@G + p0@G) instead of (p1@G + pmix@G).\n# Same number of matmuls. No saving.\n\n# Unless... we don'\"'t need p0@G as a full matrix. We only need sum(p0 * (p1@G)) for cross term.\n# cross = (p0 * p1G).sum(dim=-1) - this is just an elementwise multiply + sum, cheap.\n# But we still need norm0_sq = (p0 * (p0@G)).sum(dim=-1) which needs p0@G.\n# Unless we compute ||u0|| some other way... u0 = p0 @ x.\n# But we don't compute u0 in the v-first approach.\n# Actually... we DO know u0 @ wv\"'^T = p0 @ v. But that'\"'s D-space, not C-space norm.\n\nprint()\nprint('Key insight: p@G is 2 matmuls, projections are 3 matmuls.')\nprint('Projections dominate FLOPs but softmax is also expensive.')\nprint()\n\n# Let me check if we can do softmax more efficiently\n# Current 4D softmax is ~14ms for 2 of them. Can we do better?\nlogits = torch.randn(B, H, L, L)\n\n# Try: reshape to 2D, softmax, reshape back\ndef sm_2d(x):\n    s = x.shape\n    return x.reshape(-1, s[-1]).softmax(dim=-1).reshape(s)\n\nbench('native 4D softmax', lambda: logits.softmax(dim=-1))\nbench('via 2D reshape softmax', lambda: sm_2d(logits))\n\n# Try log_softmax then exp? No, that's slower.\n\n# What about contiguous memory layout for softmax?\n# logits is already BHLL contiguous. \n# What if it's HBLL?\nlogits_hb = logits.transpose(0,1).contiguous()  # H, B, L, L\nbench('HBLL softmax', lambda: logits_hb.softmax(dim=-1))\n\n# What if B=1 (like most cases)?\nlogits_3d = logits.squeeze(0)  # H, L, L\nbench('3D (HLL) softmax', lambda: logits_3d.softmax(dim=-1))\n\\\"\n\"","aggregated_output":"p @ G_exp (4D broadcast): 12.029 ms\np3 @ G_exp3 (3D, reshaped expand): 12.845 ms\ntorch.bmm (3D): 14.090 ms\n\nKey insight: p@G is 2 matmuls, projections are 3 matmuls.\nProjections dominate FLOPs but softmax is also expensive.\n\nnative 4D softmax: 8.068 ms\nvia 2D reshape softmax: 6.312 ms\nHBLL softmax: 6.361 ms\n3D (HLL) softmax: 6.211 ms\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
